==============================================================================
defect-2  the final safety decision is about the pathname, not the held object
==============================================================================
Timeline the guard cannot see (all three lookups are separate `stat` calls):
  1. check_target_safe(device)      name -> stick (8,32)     => permitted
  2. os.open(device)                name -> root partition (8,1)  [swap in]
  3. devnum_for_path(device)        name -> root partition (8,1)  => matches fd
  4. check_target_safe(device)      name -> stick (8,32)     [swap back] => OK
  5. dd of=/proc/self/fd/<fd>       fd     -> root partition (8,1)
The descriptor and the *last* safety decision disagree about which device is
being written.  dd is mocked here so nothing is written anywhere; the assertion
is about whether the destructive call was made at all.

--- interleaved swaps (the 5-step timeline above)
    exit status          : 0
    dd invocations      : 1
    dd output argument   : ['of=/proc/self/fd/5']
    pass_fds            : not passed
    stdout tail          : THIS ERASES EVERYTHING ON THE TARGET DEVICE.
    bytes on the device stand-in: 0
    verdict              : FAIL-OPEN: the write was authorised for a device the guard never examined

--- name pointing at the safe stick for every lookup while the fd holds the root partition
    exit status          : 1
